  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was mixed: Port Washington softened on capacity, Hicksville popped on scheduling. Neither is structural; both are tactical.\n\nPort Washington ran 4 groomers at $474/groomer\u20147% below your 30-day baseline of $509. The team is capable; you simply underdeployed relative to demand signals. Yesterday's 10% revenue dip looks worse than it is: add-on attach cratered from 61% to 45% (not a groomer problem\u2014an upsell discipline problem), and retail tanked 26% despite solid transaction count (19 vs 13 suggests a front-of-house focus gap, not inventory). Month-to-date you're only 1.1% behind DOW-adjusted expectation\u2014calendar was unfavorable, not performance. YTD you're up 54% revenue and 56% appointments versus last year; the business is scaling. But Port's lapse pool of 459 customers worth $301k annually is material\u2014that's reactivation runway.\n\nHicksville's 55% jump yesterday (2 groomers at $355, still 8% below baseline) is real. You're actually beating May expectations by 1.5% despite running lighter. Problem: add-on attach collapsed to 22% from 60%, same discipline issue as Port Washington. This is a pattern. Both stores need upsell reset. Hicksville has no bather capacity and a 38-customer lapse pool worth $25k\u2014small but urgent to address.\n\n**Capacity & coverage:** Port Washington has 0 groomers today (Sunday) and 3 tomorrow; Hicksville has 0 today and 1 tomorrow; across the next 7 days you have 40 groomer-days scheduled (Port 29, Hicksville 11) with 43 bather hours concentrated at Port."
